// THE SLOT TABLE (#5796): everything one map operation indexes, as ONE object.
// Sibling of `maps.bit` (hash, probe, grow, the ABI surface), `mapscalar.bit`
// (the four hot entry points), `mapswar.bit` and `mapcaps.bit`, same module.
//
// WHAT WAS WRONG. The header used to carry `cap`, `ctrl`, `keys` and `vals` as
// four separate words, and `mapGrow` replaced them with four plain stores. A
// reader on another worker loaded them as four plain loads, with nothing
// ordering either side, so it could pair a NEW `ctrl` with an OLD `cap` (a
// probe from the wrong home slot: a false miss), an old `cap` with a new
// `keys`, or a NEW `cap` with an OLD `ctrl` — an index up to `2*cap - 1` into a
// `cap`-byte array, which is a read past the allocation. And the comma-ok read
// is TWO calls, `bit_rt_map_slot` then `bit_rt_map_val_at`, each loading
// `vals` afresh: a grow between them indexed the NEW `vals` with the OLD slot,
// so `ok=true` came back carrying another key's value. `_tests_/stress/maprace`
// measured both on main at BIT_WORKERS=4.
//
// THE LAYOUT. A table is ONE allocation: two range words, then `cap` ctrl
// bytes, `cap` key words and `cap` value words, in that order:
//
//   [lo | hi | ctrl: cap bytes | keys: cap words | vals: cap words]
//
// `cap` is a power of two of at least 8 (mapcaps.bit), so the key words start
// 8-aligned. A table holding references is a `ref_range_info` object whose
// collector traces exactly body words `[lo, hi)` — the keys, the values, or
// both (`scanRefRange`, runtime/gc/gcmark.bit) — so tracing is as precise as
// the three separate buffers this replaced; one holding none is a leaf.
//
// ONE WORD NAMES IT, AND ONE LOAD READS EVERYTHING. The header's `mapTblW`
// packs the ctrl pointer with `64 - log2(cap)` in its top byte (`mapPack`).
// From that one load, `ctrl` is a mask, `cap - 1` is one shift of all-ones,
// and `keys` and `vals` are adds: no second load before the first ctrl byte,
// the first key word or the value word. That is the point of the layout. The
// first two cuts held a pointer to a `{cap, keys, vals}` prefix instead, one
// more L1 load in front of every probe; the second measured +5.7% to +7.3%
// cycles on Graviton3's int map for +0.67% instructions (server A/B of
// e2461f835 on #5796), a latency cost and not work. Main reached cap, ctrl
// and keys in one load level; this does too, plus two shifts. The loops bound
// themselves by `mask` rather than `cap` so the decoded `cap` is not one more
// value live across them.
//
// The header's `mapTbl` holds the table's object base as well, the one traced
// word, and exists only to keep the table alive; no operation reads it.
//
// THE INVARIANT. A table's layout is fixed by `cap`, which lives only in the
// packed word, and every array in it holds exactly `cap` slots. `mapTblW` is
// written by `mapPublish` alone (a release store, after the rehash has filled
// the new table) and read by `mapTableW` alone. So a reader that loads it ONCE
// and derives `cap`, the ctrl bytes, `keys` and `vals` from that one word gets
// a table that was complete when it was published, and every index it
// computes is `h & (cap-1)` or bounded by `< cap` of THAT table: in bounds by
// construction, whatever a writer on another worker publishes meanwhile.
// Every entry point loads the word once and threads it, or the pointers
// decoded from it, through its helpers (`mapProbe`, `mapProbeSwar`,
// `mapClaimSlot`) instead of letting them reload it.
//
// A table's slot CONTENTS are still written in place by `m[k] = v` and
// `delete`, exactly as before; what is fixed is `cap`, which is what every
// bound is computed from.
//
// THE CALL PAIR, AND WHY NO INDEX CROSSES IT. `bit_rt_map_slot` cannot hand an
// index to `bit_rt_map_val_at`: the second call would have to load the table
// again, and a grow in between moves the key to a different slot of a
// different table. No index encoding repairs that without the key, and the key
// is not an argument of `map_val_at` (ABI.md §15; adding it is an ABI change
// the pinned stage0 cannot build). So a HIT returns a TOKEN instead: the
// address of the key's value word in the table the probe used, tagged with
// `mapTokenBit`. `map_val_at` dereferences it without touching the header, so
// it reads the value word of the table the slot came from — by construction,
// not by a re-check.
//
// WHY THE TABLE IS STILL THERE. Every pointer decoded from `mapTblW`, and the
// token, is an untraced address, so each is safe exactly as long as nothing
// can free or move that table while it is held. The collector never moves an
// object (ABI.md §5). It frees only after a stop-the-world mark, and a mutator
// reaches a stop only at a back-edge poll (§5: "at loop back-edges, and
// nowhere else"). Every function here is `@nosplit` and calls nothing that
// parks, so none can be stopped while it holds a decoded pointer. Across the
// call pair, `emitMapCommaOk` (compiler/lowermapaccess.bit) emits the two
// calls back to back with no back edge between them — the adjacency contract
// (§5.1). So no collection can begin while a table is in use, and the token
// addresses `vals + i*8` with `i < cap` of that table: in bounds. The word it
// names holds a value some write actually stored for that key (SPEC §13.7) —
// the rehash copy or a later `set` on that table; once a grow has retired it,
// no `set` writes it again.
//
// ITERATION STILL PASSES INDICES. `map_iter_next` returns a plain slot index
// and the loop body runs between it and `map_key_at`/`map_val_at` — a body
// with back-edge polls, where an address would not survive. An index cannot
// collide with a token: an index is below its table's `cap`, far below 2^62,
// and a token is 2^62 plus a user address, which `mapPack` checks is below
// 2^56. So `map_val_at` tells the two apart by one bit, and the index path
// bounds-checks against the table it loads. That check is not redundant with
// "grow never shrinks": two concurrent WRITERS can each rehash the same old
// table and publish out of order, which does shrink `cap`, and a racing
// iteration must still read in bounds (SPEC §13.5 leaves its RESULT
// unspecified, never its memory safety).
//
// No imports: the helpers here use only what the root module already brings
// in (SPEC §17.1; a duplicate import is E0042).

// Bytes before the ctrl array: the two range words.
const tblHdr: int = 16

// The packed word's top byte holds `64 - log2(cap)`; the low 56 bits hold the
// ctrl pointer. Every user address on both targets is below 2^48 (2^57 only
// for an mmap hint above 2^47, which this heap never passes), and `mapPack`
// refuses one that is not below 2^56 rather than truncate it.
export const tblShift: int = 56
const tblAddrMask: int = 72057594037927935 // 2^56 - 1
const tblOnes: u64 = 0xffffffffffffffff

// 1 << 62: marks a `bit_rt_map_slot` hit token. Bit 63 stays clear, so a token
// is still `>= 0` and the compiler's `ok = slot >= 0` holds unchanged. A single
// bit, so setting, testing and clearing it are one logical-immediate
// instruction each on arm64, where an add of 2^62 needed the constant in a
// register first. `bit_rt_map_val_at` tests `slot < 0` BEFORE the bit (-1 has
// every bit set), which keeps a comma-ok MISS at main's five instructions:
// testing the bit first saved a hit two and cost each miss three, and
// bench/cases/strmap's comma-ok reads are all misses.
const mapTokenBit: int = 4611686018427387904

// The current table's packed word: the only read of `mapTblW`.
//
// A PLAIN LOAD, NOT AN ACQUIRE, and the ordering still holds. Every read a
// caller makes through it — the ctrl bytes, the key and value words, and the
// value word a `bit_rt_map_slot` token names, whose address
// `bit_rt_map_val_at` receives in a register — takes its ADDRESS from this
// load's value. Both targets order a load after the load its address depends
// on: x86-64 orders every load after every earlier load (TSO), and ARMv8
// orders an address-dependent read after the read that produced the address
// (the architecture's dependency ordering, the same guarantee Linux's
// `rcu_dereference` is a plain load for). Paired with `mapPublish`'s release,
// a reader that sees the new word sees everything written before it. The
// backend keeps that dependency: it computes each address from the loaded
// register and never predicts a loaded value. An `atomicLoad` here would be an
// `ldar` on arm64, whose RCsc ordering this path never needs.
@nosplit fn mapTableW(m: int): int {
  return loadWord(m + mapTblW)
}

// The ctrl pointer packed in `w`. One instruction; the inliner splices it.
@nosplit fn tblCtrlOf(w: int): int {
  return w & tblAddrMask
}

// `cap - 1` for the table packed in `w`: all-ones shifted right by the top
// byte. Two instructions, where `(1 << log2) - 1` took three on the path to
// the first ctrl byte.
@nosplit fn tblMaskOf(w: int): int {
  return int(tblOnes >> u64(w >> tblShift))
}

// The packed word for the table whose ctrl array is at `ctrl`, `vcap` slots.
@nosplit fn mapPack(ctrl: int, vcap: int): int {
  if ((ctrl >> tblShift) != 0) {
    panicMapAddr()
  }
  let lg = 0
  // Bounded by `tblShift`: `vcap` is a power of two, far below 2^56.
  while (lg < tblShift && (1 << lg) < vcap) {
    lg = lg + 1
  }
  return ctrl | ((64 - lg) << tblShift)
}

// Publishes the table packed in `w`: the traced base first, for the collector
// alone, then the packed word with a release store, after the table is filled.
// Neither store can be separated by a collection (see WHY THE TABLE IS STILL
// THERE), so which the collector sees first does not matter.
@nosplit fn mapPublish(m: int, w: int) {
  storeWord(m + mapTbl, tblCtrlOf(w) - tblHdr)
  atomicStoreRelease(wordAt(m + mapTblW), w)
}

// A fresh `vcap`-slot table for map `m`, every ctrl byte EMPTY and every key
// and value word 0 (`gcAllocRaw` zeroes); returns its packed word. It is a
// `ref_range_info` object tracing exactly the key words when the key
// descriptor names a reference, the value words when `mapValIsRef`, both, or
// a leaf when neither — what the three buffers it replaced traced. Exhaustion
// is fatal inside `gcAllocRaw` (root.bit constraint 3), never a returned 0.
@nosplit fn allocTable(m: int, vcap: int): int {
  let g = wordAt(gcAddr)
  let keyRef = loadWord(m + mapKeyDesc) != mapKeyScalar
  let valRef = loadWord(m + mapValIsRef) != 0
  let info = infoAddr + riSliceBuf * 8
  if (keyRef || valRef) {
    info = infoAddr + riRefRange * 8
  }
  let body = gcAllocRaw(g, tblHdr + vcap * 17, info)
  // Word indices into the body: the key words start past the range words and
  // the ctrl bytes.
  let kw = 2 + vcap / 8
  let lo = kw
  if (!keyRef) {
    lo = kw + vcap
  }
  let hi = kw + vcap * 2
  if (!valRef) {
    hi = kw + vcap
  }
  storeWord(body, lo)
  storeWord(body + 8, hi)
  return mapPack(body + tblHdr, vcap)
}

// The token `bit_rt_map_slot` returns for a hit at slot `i`, `vals` being the
// value array of the table the probe used.
@nosplit fn mapValToken(vals: int, i: int): int {
  return (vals + i * 8) | mapTokenBit
}

// The "map table addr" panic, for an address `mapPack` cannot pack. Built in
// `nilMapMsg` (maps.bit) for the reason that buffer exists: the panic path must
// not allocate. NEVER RETURNS.
const mapAddrWord0: int = 7809911855202197869
const mapAddrWord1: int = 125775506382949
const mapAddrLen: int = 14

@nosplit fn panicMapAddr() {
  let base = int(ptrOf(nilMapMsg))
  let text = base + strHeaderSize
  storeWord(base + strPtr, text)
  storeWord(base + strLen, mapAddrLen)
  storeWord(text + 0, mapAddrWord0)
  storeWord(text + 8, mapAddrWord1)
  bit_rt_panic(base)
}
